"use strict";(self.webpackChunksaferoceanave=self.webpackChunksaferoceanave||[]).push([[98],{98(e,n,a){a.r(n),a.d(n,{default:()=>o});var t=a(540),i=a(556);function r({ref:e=null}){return t.createElement("div",{ref:e,className:"markdown",dangerouslySetInnerHTML:{__html:"<details>\n    <summary>\n        What is the Safer Ocean Avenue campaign?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>The Safer Ocean Avenue campaign was started in January 2026 by volunteer\nresidents and homeowners in the neigborhoods along Ocean Avenue, like Ingleside\nand Westwood Park. The campaign is supported by San Francisco Transit Riders\n(SFTR), in response to the delay of implementing the red-transit only lanes on\nOcean Avenue. The campaign&#39;s goals are to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>advocate for SFMTA to deliver safety now, with the completion of the Rapid K\nproject as originally approved;</li>\n<li>improve Ocean Avenue&#39;s safety and accessibility for people who want to visit,\nnot just drive through; and</li>\n<li>leverage the investments already made, not waste them with delays that put\nfamilies at greater risk.</details></li>\n</ul>\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        What is the purpose of the red transit-only lane for Ocean Avenue?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>It is a dedicated lane for public transit, specifically for the K-Ingleside\nmetro train. This will make transit run more safely, reliably, and quickly.</p>\n<p>Safety is also a key purpose. Ocean Avenue is on the high-injury network;\nelsewhere in San Francisco, transit lanes have helped reduce speeding and other\nrisky driving.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        What about emergency vehicles? Won't this impede their ability to travel through\n        Ocean Avenue?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>Emergency vehicles can freely use red lanes. Since the red lane will have\nconsiderably fewer vehicles, emergency vehicles will be able to travel faster\nalong Ocean Ave.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        Are they going to remove parking when the red transit lanes are painted?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>No, red lanes on Ocean Ave are not related to any parking changes.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        How has Ocean Ave parking been affected?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>As of January 2026, the project has added 20 new parking spaces, removed 25\nparking spaces for boarding island extensions (for 2-car K trains), and\nconverted 17 spots into loading zones. The last boarding island extension will\nremove 4 more spots (Ocean/Miramar inbound, estimated early 2027).</p>\n<p>None of these changes are related to the red lanes.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        Why are we painting red lanes now? Can't we just keep things the way\n        they are?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>The red lanes are part of the Ocean Avenue Mobility Plan started in late 2021.\nThe plan improves transit while trying to address the corridor being on San\nFrancisco&#39;s High Injury Network: areas with the highest number of serious\ntraffic-related injuries or fatalities.</p>\n<p>With the Balboa Reservoir development opening in Spring 2028, the neighborhood\nis also expecting several thousand more residents. More people means a higher\nrisk of traffic collisions. We need to prepare now to safely move around Ocean\nAvenue.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        If safety is a concern, what do red transit-only lanes have to do with\n        it?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>Red lanes are part of the comprehensive mobility and traffic safety plan. Much\nof this plan has already been built: new no-left-turn restrictions, platform\nextensions for 2-car K trains, loading zones, signage updates, new left turn\nsignal at Brighton, and new angled parking on Dorado.</p>\n<p>However, the whole plan falls apart without the red lanes. They serve as a\nvisual deterrent that helps the majority of drivers drive safely and slower. In\nsimilar corridors, they&#39;ve reduced collisions by 16% and injury collisions by\n24%.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        Ocean Avenue is notorious for double parking. If the red lanes are\n        there, how will that make things flow better?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>Drivers can still briefly enter the red lanes to get around double-parking and\nother blockages, or to make a left turn. Striped red-lanes will be painted in\nthe initial segments to encourage a smooth transition, and will also remind\ndrivers where left turns are OK.</p>\n<p>The SFMTA is also working with merchants to identify parking and loading changes\nthat could help reduce double parking.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        What about congestion? Will it make Ocean Ave undriveable and push cars into the adjacent neighborhoods, increasing car traffic there?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>SFMTA will be monitoring the impacts of the phased approach and adjust if\ntraffic slowed down too much, or too many vehicles stopped using Ocean Avenue.\nIt will also simplify driving on Ocean Ave; drivers will not need to deal with\nweaving in and out of traffic.</p>\n<p>The single lane encourages drivers to respect the existing speed limit of 20mph.\nSpeed is a primary cause of severe injuries or death from traffic collisions.\nAccording to the Federal Highway Administration, if a driver at 20mph hits a\nperson, the pedestrian has a 95% chance to survive. At 30mph, their chance to\nsurvive drops drastically to 55%; at 40mph, it is just 15%.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        What about local businesses? How will they be impacted by the red lanes?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>We have not seen any data-driven evidence showing a negative impact to\ncommercial corridors from red lanes. Sales tax revenues on Taraval, Mission, and\nGeary, where red lanes exist, performed better than the city average. A reliable\nK-Ingleside and safer Ocean Ave will bring in more riders and pedestrians, each\na potential new or repeat customer.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        Isn't SFMTA running out of money? Why would we want to spend on this\n        when Muni needs money?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>SFMTA has two types of funding: operations and capital. Operations is what pays\nfor running the system, maintenance, and payroll. Capital budgets are for\none-time infrastructure projects, such as the changes on Ocean Ave.</p>\n<p>Years of public process and public money are already committed and spent on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the 2021 SFCTA study;</li>\n<li>SFMTA public consultation (office hours, surveys, mailers);</li>\n<li>the 2024 board approval; and</li>\n<li>construction already underway since late 2024 (platform extensions, loading zones, signal work).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stalling or re-litigating now wastes the planning, engineering, and construction\ndollars already spent.</p>\n</details>\n\n<details>\n    <summary>\n        Will the construction disrupt Ocean Avenue like it did for Taraval and\n        Van Ness?\n    </summary>\n\n<p>Most of the construction has already been completed, such as the extension of\nthe loading islands. Installing the red lane will take several nights when the\ntrain is not running to minimize disruption.</p>\n</details>\n"}})}function s({toggle:e}){return t.createElement("div",{className:"detailsControls-VZPR2"},t.createElement("button",{className:"success-OL8m_",onClick:n=>e(n,!0)},"+ Show all"),t.createElement("button",{className:"danger-dahQc",onClick:n=>e(n,!1)},"- Hide all"))}function o(){const e=(0,t.useRef)(null);function n(n,a){e.current.querySelectorAll("details").forEach(e=>{a?e.setAttribute("open",""):e.removeAttribute("open")})}return t.createElement(t.Fragment,null,t.createElement("h1",null,"Myths, facts, and frequently-asked questions"),t.createElement(s,{toggle:n}),t.createElement(r,{ref:e}),t.createElement(s,{toggle:n}))}a.dn(r),s.propTypes={toggle:i.func.isRequired}}}]);
